    <h1>Sleep During Pregnancy: Why It Changes and How to Cope</h1>

    <p>Sleep problems are one of the most common complaints in pregnancy. Most pregnant people experience some degree of sleep disturbance, and it tends to be most pronounced in the third trimester. The good news: there are practical steps that help, and most sleep changes are normal and resolve after birth.</p>

    <h2>Why Sleep Changes During Pregnancy</h2>

    <p>Several overlapping factors disrupt sleep during pregnancy. Often more than one is at work at the same time.</p>

    <h3>Hormonal changes</h3>
    <p>Progesterone rises sharply in early pregnancy and is a potent sedative. It is the main reason many people feel exhausted and need more sleep in the first trimester. Later in pregnancy, hormonal shifts (including rising estrogen and progesterone) alter sleep architecture and can reduce restorative deep sleep.</p>

    <h3>Physical discomfort</h3>
    <p>As the uterus grows, finding a comfortable position becomes harder. Breast tenderness, back pain, and round-ligament pain all make it more difficult to settle and stay asleep.</p>

    <h3>Frequent urination</h3>
    <p>The uterus presses on the bladder, and the kidneys process more fluid during pregnancy. This means more trips to the bathroom, especially at night, and it often worsens in the third trimester when the baby's head engages.</p>

    <h3>Heartburn and reflux</h3>
    <p>Progesterone relaxes the lower esophageal sphincter, allowing stomach acid to rise. Lying flat makes reflux worse, so many people wake with burning or regurgitation.</p>

    <h3>Leg cramps and restless legs</h3>
    <p>Nocturnal leg cramps are common in the second and third trimesters. Restless legs syndrome also appears more often in pregnancy and can delay sleep onset.</p>

    <h3>Anxiety and racing thoughts</h3>
    <p>Worry about the baby, birth, and the life changes ahead is normal, and it is a well-documented cause of insomnia in pregnancy.</p>

    <p><em>Sources: NHS, https://www.nhs.uk/pregnancy/common-symptoms/tiredness/; ACOG; Mayo Clinic.</em></p>

    <h2>Sleep Hygiene in Pregnancy</h2>

    <p>Sleep hygiene is the habits and surroundings that make sleep more likely. The NHS suggests keeping the bedroom dark and quiet, using an eye mask or ear plugs if needed, and going to bed and waking at the same time every day. Avoid caffeine after midday, and avoid heavy meals and strenuous exercise late at night.</p>

    <p><em>Sources: NHS, https://www.nhs.uk/pregnancy/common-symptoms/tiredness/.</em></p>

    <AdUnit slot="925728676" format="auto" />
    <h2>Sleep Changes by Trimester</h2>

    <h3>First Trimester (Weeks 1-13)</h3>
    <p>Fatigue is the dominant feature. Many people need significantly more sleep than usual and may nap during the day. Paradoxically, nausea and vomiting can still interrupt sleep. The overall pattern is often "sleepy but not well rested."</p>

    <h3>Second Trimester (Weeks 14-27)</h3>
    <p>For many, this is the most comfortable stretch of pregnancy. Energy often returns, nausea fades, and sleep may improve. Reflux and frequent urination can still interrupt nights, but the third-trimester sleep disruption has usually not yet begun.</p>

    <h3>Third Trimester (Weeks 28-40)</h3>
    <p>Sleep disturbance peaks here. A large uterus makes positioning difficult, reflux and frequent urination intensify, and the baby's movements can wake you. Insomnia is common, and total sleep time often drops even as the need for rest rises.</p>

    <p><em>Sources: NHS, https://www.nhs.uk/pregnancy/common-symptoms/tiredness/; ACOG; NICHD; Mayo Clinic.</em></p>

    <h2>Safe Sleep Positions</h2>

    <p>Sleeping on your side, particularly the <strong>left side</strong>, is recommended in pregnancy, especially in the second and third trimesters.</p>

    <p>Why side sleeping matters: lying on your left side improves blood flow and nutrient delivery to the placenta and baby, and it relieves pressure on the inferior vena cava, the large vein that returns blood to the heart and which the heavy uterus can compress when you lie flat on your back.</p>

    <p><strong>Avoid sleeping flat on your back in the third trimester.</strong> Research has linked going to sleep on the back in late pregnancy with an increased risk of stillbirth. The absolute risk remains small, but because the finding is consistent across multiple studies, guidance bodies advise settling to sleep on the side instead.</p>

    <p>Practical tips for side sleeping:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Use a pillow between your knees to align your hips and reduce back strain.</li>
      <li>Place a pillow under your belly for support.</li>
      <li>Use a pillow behind your back to keep you from rolling onto your back.</li>
      <li>A full-body pregnancy pillow can make side sleeping much more comfortable.</li>
      <li>If you wake on your back, simply roll onto your side, and there is no need to worry.</li>
    </ul>

    <p><em>Sources: NHS, https://www.nhs.uk/pregnancy/common-symptoms/tiredness/; ACOG; Auckland Stillbirth Study and subsequent research.</em></p>

    <h2>Setting Up Pillows for Side Sleeping</h2>

    <p>Side sleeping is easier to sustain when your body is supported in a few key places. Place a pillow between your knees to keep your hips level, and one under your bump so the weight of the uterus is not pulling on your back. A pillow tucked behind your back makes rolling onto your back less likely.</p>

    <p><em>Sources: NHS, https://www.nhs.uk/pregnancy/common-symptoms/tiredness/.</em></p>

    <h2>Napping in Pregnancy</h2>

    <p>Tiredness is one of the most common symptoms of early pregnancy, and resting more is the usual answer. The NHS advises making time to sit with your feet up during the day and accepting offers of help from colleagues and family. Timing matters more than the nap itself, and the NHS advises against napping in the afternoon or evening, as this can leave you not tired at bedtime.</p>

    <p><em>Sources: NHS, https://www.nhs.uk/pregnancy/common-symptoms/tiredness/.</em></p>

    <h2>Restless Legs and Leg Cramps at Night</h2>

    <p>Restless legs syndrome is a strong urge to move your legs, usually when resting at night. The NHS describes uncomfortable feelings in the legs, such as tingling, throbbing or itching, and sometimes pain. Symptoms are usually worse at night and can affect your sleep. Restless legs can be linked to pregnancy, and symptoms usually go away after giving birth.</p>

    <p>Leg cramps are common and usually harmless. The NHS says a muscle in the leg tightens and causes a sudden pain that can make it hard to move. They can last from a few seconds to 10 minutes and usually affect the calf. Stretching and massaging the muscle during a cramp may ease the pain. The NHS advises seeing a GP if cramps are disturbing your sleep or last longer than 10 minutes.</p>

    <p><em>Sources: NHS, https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/restless-legs-syndrome/; NHS, https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/leg-cramps/.</em></p>


