    <h2>Tips for Better Sleep</h2>

    <ul>
      <li><strong>Stick to a schedule:</strong> Go to bed and wake at consistent times, even on weekends.</li>
      <li><strong>Sleep on your side:</strong> Left side is best, but either side is fine.</li>
      <li><strong>Use pillows strategically:</strong> Between the knees, under the belly, behind the back.</li>
      <li><strong>Manage heartburn:</strong> Eat smaller meals, avoid lying down for 1-2 hours after eating, and elevate the head of your bed or use extra pillows.</li>
      <li><strong>Time your fluids:</strong> Stay well hydrated during the day, but reduce fluids in the hour or two before bed.</li>
      <li><strong>Move during the day:</strong> Gentle exercise (with your clinician's approval) improves sleep, just avoid vigorous activity close to bedtime.</li>
      <li><strong>Wind down:</strong> Limit screens before bed, keep the room cool and dark, and try a calming routine.</li>
      <li><strong>Limit caffeine:</strong> Especially in the afternoon and evening.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Sleep Medicines and Supplements</h2>

    <p>Do not take any sleep medication or sleep supplement during pregnancy without asking your clinician first. This includes products bought from a pharmacy or a shop, and herbal or "natural" products. A product being available to buy does not mean it is established as safe in pregnancy. Your clinician can weigh any possible benefit against the risk for you and your baby.</p>

    <p><em>Sources: NHS, https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/restless-legs-syndrome/.</em></p>

    <h2>When to Talk to Your Clinician</h2>

    <p>Some sleep changes are normal, but certain symptoms deserve medical attention. Contact your healthcare provider if:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Insomnia is severe or persistent and affecting your daily functioning</li>
      <li>You (or your partner) notice loud snoring, gasping, or pauses in breathing during sleep, as this can indicate sleep apnea, which is more common in pregnancy and needs assessment</li>
      <li>Restless legs are frequent, unsettling, and stopping you from sleeping</li>
      <li>Leg cramps are disturbing your sleep, or you also have numbness or swelling in your legs</li>
      <li>You have symptoms of depression or anxiety, or persistent low mood</li>
      <li>You are considering any sleep medication or supplement</li>
      <li>You are concerned about your sleep for any other reason</li>
    </ul>

    <p>Sleep apnea in particular is under-recognized in pregnancy. It is more likely in people with obesity or chronic high blood pressure, and it is associated with complications that benefit from treatment. If you suspect it, ask your clinician rather than dismissing it as "just pregnancy."</p>

    <p><em>Sources: NHS, https://www.nhs.uk/pregnancy/common-symptoms/tiredness/; ACOG.</em></p>

